# Assistant team usage

## Quick start after provider authentication

```bash
~/team create-project my-project
${EDITOR:-vi} ~/projects/my-project/assistant-prompt.md
~/team start my-project
~/team attach my-project orchestrator
```

Tell the orchestrator what to do. In another terminal: `~/team watch my-project`.
Stop with `~/team stop my-project`. No manual target creation is needed.

## Enter the runtime account

From the VM's automation account:

```bash
sudo -iu {{ containers.runtime_user }}
export XDG_RUNTIME_DIR="/run/user/$(id -u)"
export DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS="unix:path=$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/bus"
```

All following commands run as this account on the VM. The exports fix access to
its systemd user bus after a sudo login; they are not provider credentials.

## Authenticate once in each account/provider directory

```bash
cd ~/claude-work && ./login
cd ~/claude-priv && ./login
cd ~/gemini && ./login
cd ~/codex && ./login
cd ~/mistral && ./login
```

Claude work/private use separate native `claude auth login` flows. Gemini opens
its native Sign in with Google flow with a remote browser link; it does not
require gcloud. Codex uses native device authentication and a local file store.
Mistral opens Vibe's native setup wizard; choose the intended subscription/account
rather than introducing a paid API fallback. Complete login yourself; no login,
model request, or account terms were accepted during installation.

Actual native executables are available as `./claude`, `./gemini`, `./codex` and
`./vibe` in their respective directories. They run directly on Linux, not through
login containers. Program artifacts, HOME, provider settings, credentials, caches,
state and temporary files are scoped inside each directory. Work/private Claude
share only immutable program files, never credentials or configuration. Update
pinned program versions through Ansible.

On explicit role launch, only missing provider credential files are seeded into
the role's isolated home. Other settings, hooks, sessions and workspaces are not
shared. Existing role credentials retain their native refresh state. Actual
provider authentication/refresh behavior is verified only after your login;
subscription exhaustion must not switch automatically to paid API usage.

## Create and run a project

```bash
cd ~
./team create-project my-project
${EDITOR:-vi} ~/projects/my-project/assistant-prompt.md
./team start my-project
./team status my-project
./team attach my-project orchestrator
./team watch my-project
./team stop my-project
./team restart my-project
```

Project names use lowercase letters/digits, underscores or hyphens (1–40
characters, starting with a letter/digit). Creation initializes the stock SQLite
schema and eight role profiles without starting containers or models. Each project
has its own `db.sqlite`, `assistant-prompt.md`, role homes/workspaces and containers
under `~/projects/NAME`. Existing projects and edited prompts are not overwritten.
The prompt file starts with the project goal/context and has all eight role sections.
Only that goal and the selected role section enter a container.

Authenticate Claude work/private, Codex and Gemini first. Start checks saved
subscription/OAuth authentication for every role before starting any project
service, then launches all eight native harnesses into their tmux sessions without
an initial task prompt. This checks saved credential format, not remote token
validity: expired/revoked credentials can still require native reauthentication.
Mistral is optional and is not assigned a team role. Native onboarding, workspace
trust and Codex hook approvals remain explicit; no security prompts are bypassed.

Direct systemd equivalents are:

```bash
systemctl --user start assistant-connector@my-project.target
systemctl --user stop assistant-connector@my-project.target
systemctl --user restart assistant-connector@my-project.target
systemctl --user status assistant-connector@my-project.target
```

Project targets are not automatically enabled at boot. Stop affects only the named
project and interrupts its live native processes while preserving SQLite, role
homes, credentials and workspace files. Restart starts new native CLI sessions;
conversation history remains on disk but automatic native conversation resume
is not claimed. Detach tmux with Ctrl-b then d. Model activity begins when native
CLI interactions request it; subscription exhaustion must not switch to paid APIs.

Project prompt/review commands:

```bash
./team apply-prompts my-project
./team refresh-observer my-project
./team deliver-observer-report my-project
./team launch my-project builder
```

Only five collaboration roles join each project worker broker. The hidden observer
and the separate private/corporate connector containers are outside it. Observer
refresh/report delivery remains manual and project-scoped; connector general
tools remain disabled and external scoped connector services remain unconfigured.
The live viewer uses the stock event subscription, not timed inbox polling.

The initial VM has limited RAM and disk space. Parallel active projects multiply
native processes/workspaces, while reusing the installed images and vendor logins.
Resource capacity for multiple busy teams is not yet verified.

## Project-only control

The original non-project target/services and containers are retired. Use a project
name with every team lifecycle, attach, viewer and prompt command. There is no
all-projects master target. Saved original profile homes, prompt defaults and
SQLite data are retained; project creation reuses only the profile specifications
and editable prompt defaults, never the original database or provider state.

After authentication, `./team start NAME` launches all eight native sessions;
`./team attach NAME orchestrator` opens the orchestrator's tmux pane. Other roles
are builder, investigator, challenger, summarizer, observer, private-connector and
corporate-connector. Claude uses claude-work except the Private Connector's
claude-priv. Builder uses Codex and challenger Gemini. An already active role
must be attached rather than launched again.

## Edit project prompts and deliver private review

```bash
${EDITOR:-vi} ~/projects/my-project/assistant-prompt.md
./team apply-prompts my-project
./team refresh-observer my-project
./team attach my-project observer
./team deliver-observer-report my-project
```

Keep exactly one `# Role: name` header for each of the eight roles. Instructions
include only the project goal and selected role section; hidden observer identity
is excluded from worker instructions. Prompt changes take effect on next native
launch. The global ~/assistant-prompts.md remains the editable seed for newly
created projects and is not mounted into containers.

Refresh provides the observer a read-only snapshot. Ask it to write report.md in
its workspace; delivery copies it only to the orchestrator's private-review.md.
This remains a manual bridge, not continuous evaluation or automatic reassignment.
Connector roles have no external mail/calendar tools or execution audit yet.
Do not import edited prompts or native authentication into Git without applying
the repository's sensitive-data/Vault policy.

## Reproduce and verify

The private repository's `src/configure_agents_connector.yml` calls separate roles
for the Podman host, connector setup, native local authentication installations
and the project framework. `--tags project_framework` updates project controls
and instance unit templates without restarting existing sessions.
`--tags native_auth,auth_sources` updates local installations and prompt/auth sources
without restarting the service group. Offline model-free checks cover native
versions/help, constrained containers, hooks, message delivery and event wake.
The opt-in validate_projects.py uses disposable projects and dummy harnesses
without provider/model calls; it checks event streaming and project isolation.
